PicoGKRuntime 26.2 (0f26321c) — render lattices through openvdb's parallel
tube-complex voxelizer instead of the serial dense-accessor loop.

PROPOSAL, NOT APPLIED LOCALLY. picovoxel ships this as a sibling translation
unit (src/pico-lattice.cpp -> Voxels_RenderLatticeTubes, the facade default
since SK-0.4, with `createPico({ serialLattice: true })` as the escape hatch)
so the vendored tree stays pristine and the old export stays reachable
byte-for-byte. This file is the same algorithm shaped as a diff for
leap71/PicoGKRuntime, where it belongs behind the existing entry point. Ledger
row U5.

PREREQUISITE — patches/PicoGKRuntime/0003-lattice-parameter-accessors.patch
(seven `inline ... const` getters on LatticeSphere/LatticeBeam: vecCenter,
fRadius, vecStart, vecEnd, fRadStart, fRadEnd, bRoundCap). That patch is a pure
insertion, adds no member and changes no declaration, and is the other half of
the same U5 ask. This diff APPLIES to a pristine tree but only COMPILES with
those accessors present: without them `fSdValue()` is the only readable surface
on a beam, which forces every renderer into exactly the per-sample shape being
replaced here.

WHY. Voxels::RenderLattice walks every sphere and every beam in turn and, for
each, walks its padded bounding box voxel by voxel through ONE
FloatGrid::Accessor, taking min(element SDF, existing value). One thread, one
accessor, one element at a time — and a lattice is authored in the hundreds of
thousands of elements (one picovoxel fixture, LEAP71_HelixHeatX, renders
1,197,460 beams across 37 lattices). openvdb has shipped the parallel
replacement since 12.1: tools::createLevelSetTubeComplex (LevelSetTubes.h —
already #included by this very file, line 49, unused) takes vertex, segment and
radius ARRAYS and voxelizes them with a tbb reduce over spatially bucketed
segments, each bucket driving ConvexVoxelizer's scanline walk over only the
voxels the shape actually touches; per-thread trees are combined with a
node-stealing csg union.

The mapping is exact rather than approximate, which is the point: a round-capped
PicoGK beam IS the convex hull of its two end spheres, and so is a tapered
capsule. The full case table, including the two cases that do not map, is in the
doc comment on bRenderLatticeTubes() below.

MEASURED (picovoxel SK-0.4, bench/results/webgpu-v2/SK-0.4.md; wasm/12T, paired
and order-alternated, 12 repeats, bootstrap CI on the median log ratio):

  - HelixHeatX's two largest creation stages together (turning-fins 355.6k beams
    + helical-void 65k beams): 8380.4 -> 1384.9 ms = 6.05x at 1.0 mm, and
    13.2x at 0.5 mm. Per fixture at 1.0 mm: turning-fins 3.24x [3.18, 3.29],
    straight-fins 3.38x, helical-void 19.11x [18.88, 19.23], a quasicrystal
    wireframe 7.14x. At 0.5 mm: 5.55x / 6.03x / 34.97x / 14.26x;
  - SINGLE-THREADED, the algorithmic delta alone is 2.57-4.23x — the scanline
    voxelizer beats the dense bounding-box walk before any thread is added;
  - flat-capped lattices (the serial fallback) time at 1.00x [0.995, 0.997] —
    the fallback costs nothing;
  - memory is the price: +61.6 MiB peak at 10^5 beams (112.8 vs 51.2 MiB heap
    growth at 0.25 mm), for the complex's own grid plus the per-thread trees and
    the combine staging. That growth is identical single-vs-multi, because the
    deterministic split tree fixes the number of intermediate trees regardless
    of worker count.

DETERMINISM IS BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY LUCK — and this is the one place the
patch deliberately departs from upstream's own entry point. createLevelSetTube-
Complex drives the voxelizer under tbb::parallel_reduce with the auto
partitioner, so the number of body splits, and therefore the shape of the csg
join tree, depends on how many workers turn up. csg union is a min, and min is
exactly associative and commutative in IEEE, so the VALUES are order-free either
way; the tree TOPOLOGY is not. This patch drives the same voxelizer under
tbb::parallel_deterministic_reduce with a grain size derived only from the
bucket count, so the split tree is a pure function of the input — identical at 1
thread and at 12. Measured: 3/3 run-to-run stable AND single == multi identical
(raw level-set volume as hex double, mesh vertex/triangle counts, FNV-1a over
the vertex and triangle byte arrays) on all five fixtures at 0.5 mm. It costs
nothing; the timings above are all on the deterministic driver.

DIVERGENCE CLASS (picovoxel's taxonomy, NON-DETERMINISM.md §1: 0 = byte
identical, 1 = same geometry / permuted bytes, 2 = bounded numeric drift,
X = wrong geometry):

  - run-to-run, and single-thread vs multi-thread: CLASS 0, byte-identical, by
    the construction above and measured 3/3 at 0.5 mm;
  - versus the serial lane it replaces: CLASS 2. The two lanes compute the same
    shapes through different narrow-band constructions, so the fields are close
    but not identical. Across 17 equivalence fixtures at 0.5 mm (SK-0.8 gate
    shape: vol/area rel <= 3%, bounds <= 1 voxel, band SDF max <= 1 voxel /
    mean <= 0.25 voxel, tools::checkLevelSet, mesh validity) every gate passes,
    with max narrow-band SDF delta 9.8e-6 mm, mean 1.3e-7 mm, bounds delta 0,
    and volume moves on picovoxel's byte-locked corpus of at most 2.3e-5
    relative. Several cases (sphere, tapered r 3->1, zero-length round beam,
    every flat-capped fixture) are bit-exact. A reviewer should expect a
    byte-pinned corpus to move by that much, and to move once.
  - tools::checkLevelSet, read correctly for lattices: the SERIAL reference is
    not clean — the per-element min-splat leaves |grad(phi)| violations wherever
    two elements overlap (turning-fins reference: 458,402 voxels). On every
    fixture the tube complex's diagnosis is byte-identical to the reference's,
    except on nested end spheres where the complex is EMPTY and the reference has
    160 violations. The lane never makes the field worse, and once makes it
    strictly better.

ONE CORRECTNESS FIX RIDES ALONG (picovoxel ledger row U23), and it is not
drift: when a beam's end spheres nest, (p0-p1)^2 <= (r0-r1)^2, fSdvRoundCone
computes a2 = l^2 - (r0-r1)^2 as a negative number and takes sqrtf of a negative
product. Against the closed form for r 6->1 the serial lane renders -90.7% of
the true volume; the tube complex renders -0.37% and is checkLevelSet-clean.
Where the two lanes disagree materially, the new one is the correct one.

WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. The exported C ABI is untouched: Voxels_RenderLattice
keeps its signature and its semantics (union into the existing grid), so the
PicoGK C# binding is a drop-in. No new dependency: LevelSetTubes.h is already
included by this file, and Prune.h / the two tbb headers already arrive
transitively through Composite.h — they are spelled out only so the file states
what it uses.

SHAPE OF THE CHANGE. RenderLattice gains a five-line dispatch at its head and
keeps its serial body verbatim as the fallback; the new work is one private
method. The recursion in that dispatch terminates at depth one by construction:
the flat-capped remainder is authored with bRoundCap = false and contains no
spheres, so the second call finds nothing the complex can express and takes the
false branch. The remainder is rendered by exactly the loop that renders it
today.

COMPOSITION with the rest of the picovoxel series (all verified by
`patch -p1 --fuzz=0` dry-run plus a full Release build of the composed tree):

  - 0003 lattice parameter accessors — REQUIRED (see PREREQUISITE above);
  - 0004 lattice beam value storage (std::vector<LatticeBeam::Ptr> becomes
    std::vector<LatticeBeam>) — OPTIONAL, and deliberately so. This patch reads
    the beam array through two `oBeamRef` overloads, one for the value form and
    one for the shared_ptr form, so it compiles unchanged on either storage and
    neither patch has to land before the other;
  - 0001 parallel disjoint mesh flatten, 0002 post-fill prune — no interaction.
    0002 appends PruneFill() to the end of the serial RenderLattice body; the
    tube path returns before it and does not need it, because the complex is
    pruned on construction and csgUnion prunes on exit. In the mixed
    flat-capped case the recursive serial call reaches 0002's PruneFill()
    normally.

Applies with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree, and
onto that tree with picovoxel's patches/PicoGKRuntime/0001-0004 already applied.

